curacy class varies per size (some have both classes with different flow rates), the model variant is really size+class combination.\n\nLet me think about what's most useful. The tables vary by two axes: meter size and accuracy class. The flow rates depend on both. So model_id like \"DN40/class 1\". Or I could keep models as DN sizes and use config_level for accuracy class. Hmm.\n\nActually the accuracy class axis: for DN65 etc. it's \"1 & 2\" meaning both classes share the same values. For DN40 etc. class 1 and class 2 have different Q1/Q2/ratio. So a 2-D structure: rows = size × class.\n\nI think listing models as \"DN40 class 1\", \"DN40 class 2\", etc. is cleanest for model_level. But then the two tables (AC mains full bore vs battery full bore) have the same sizes but different... wait, Table 5 is full bore battery powered. The unnamed table (continuation, likely full bore AC mains) — its row DN1800 has same format. Tables 4 (AC mains, presumably) and 5 (battery) may have same or different values per size. The fragment only shows DN1800 from the AC mains table.\n\nHmm, so the powering mode (AC mains vs battery/renewable) is another config axis affecting indicating device characteristics (Tables 6-9). And bore type (reduced vs full) is another axis.\n\nGiven the complexity, I think the best approach:\n\n- model_family.models: entries per meter size (DN40 through DN1800), each with attributes: bore type availability? Actually some sizes only appear in full bore (DN700+ don't appear in reduced bore tables... let me check: Table 6 reduced bore AC: DN40-DN600. Table 7 reduced bore battery: DN40-DN600. Table 8 full bore AC: DN40-DN1800. So DN700+ only full bore.) Hmm, but that's inferring. Actually reduced bore tables only go to DN600, full bore goes to DN1800.\n\nAlternatively models = size × class × powering... too complex.\n\nLet me simplify: I'll use matrix_tables to faithfully preserve the four/five tables, and model_level characteristics for the flow rates keyed by model_id \"DN40 class 1\" etc.\n\nActually wait — should model_level entries be keyed per power mode? Table 5 (full bore battery) and the unnamed table (full bore AC mains presumably) — the DN1800 row appears only in the AC mains table (as the last size). It's possible the AC mains table (Table 4, on page 6-7 presumably) has the same values as Table 5 for shared sizes, or different. We can't know from this fragment. Since the fragment only gives one row of the unnamed table, I'll include it as a matrix_table named appropriately (e.g., \"Flow rates by meter size — Full bore – AC mains powered (continuation)\") and model_level values with model_id \"DN1800 class 1&2\".\n\nHmm, but for consistency, maybe model IDs should be like \"DN1800\" with accuracy class as attribute? Let me reconsider.\n\nOption A: models per size+class: model_id \"DN40 class 1\". Attributes at model_level: q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio, accuracy_class. That gives ~49 model entries. That's a lot of JSON but doable.\n\nOption B: models per size (36 sizes DN40...DN1800), model_level entries for q1/q2/q3/q4 with values keyed by \"DN40 (class 1)\" — but then model field needs to be a model_id that exists.\n\nOption A is more consistent. Let me enumerate all models across both flow tables:\n\nUnnamed table (AC mains full bore, partial):\n1. DN1800 class 1&2\n\nTable 5 (battery full bore):\n2. DN40 class 1\n3. DN40 class 2\n4. DN50 class 1\n5. DN50 class 2\n6. DN65 class 1&2\n7. DN80 class 1&2\n8. DN100 class 1&2\n9. DN125 class 1&2\n10. DN150 class 1\n11. DN150 class 2\n12. DN200 class 1\n13. DN200 class 2\n14. DN250 class 1\n15. DN250 class 2\n16. DN300 class 1\n17. DN300 class 2\n18. DN350 class 1&2\n19. DN400 class 1&2\n20. DN450 class 1&2\n21. DN500 class 1&2\n22. DN600 class 1\n23. DN600 class 2\n24. DN700 class 1\n25. DN700 class 2\n26. DN750 class 1\n27. DN750 class 2\n28. DN800 class 1\n29. DN800 class 2\n30. DN900 class 2\n31. DN1000 class 2\n32. DN1050 class 2\n33. DN1100 class 2\n34. DN1