of instruments\". I'll skip description to avoid fabrication. Actually, a small description could help context but rule 8 says omit if not present. Omit.\n\n**model_family:**\n- family_name: null (or omit). Schema: include only if cert covers >1 model variant — it does. family_name: null since no explicit name.\n- models[]: 40 entries (39 from Table 5 + 1 DN1800 row from the unnamed table). Each model: {\"model_id\": \"DN40 class 1\"}. Should I add attributes like accuracy_class and power? The models appear in \"Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered\" table; the DN1800 row is from the AC mains table (presumably). Ugh, mixing.\n\nHmm, alternative cleaner approach: models per meter size (36 sizes), with attributes {accuracy_classes: [...], powering: [...]}. And model_level uses model_id = size for attributes that are class-independent... but flow rates ARE class-dependent for many sizes.\n\nLet me think about what's most robust for a downstream merger: parts 1, 2, 4 will contain other tables (reduced bore flow tables on earlier pages probably, type designations, etc.). The merger will combine. For model_level, keying by \"DN40 class 1\" is unambiguous. I'll go with that.\n\nActually hmm, wait. Let me reconsider using model_id like \"DN40 (class 1)\" vs \"DN40/class 1\". I'll use \"DN40 class 1\" — simple.\n\nAnd for DN1800 from the unnamed table: \"DN1800 class 1&2\".\n\nmodel_level attributes (from flow tables):\n- minimum_flow_rate_q1 (unit m³/h)\n- transitional_flow_rate_q2 (m³/h)\n- permanent_flow_rate_q3 (m³/h)\n- overload_flow_rate_q4 (m³/h)\n- ratio_q3_q1 (dimensionless)\n\nValues per model. 40 models each. That's 5×40 = 200 entries. Plus accuracy_class? The class is in the model_id. I could also add a \"nominal_size_dn\" attribute with unit mm — DN refers to nominal diameter in mm. That's 40 more entries. Hmm, maybe overkill but useful. Actually DN number is already in the model_id. Skip.\n\nNow — but wait, is the flow-rate table per powering mode? Ta