20000, ratio=40\nDN1500 class 2: Q1=400, Q2=640, Q3=16000, Q4=20000, ratio=40\nDN1600 class 2: Q1=400, Q2=640, Q3=16000, Q4=20000, ratio=40\nDN1650 class 2: Q1=400, Q2=640, Q3=16000, Q4=20000, ratio=40\n\nPlus DN1800 class 1&2: Q1=317.5, Q2=507.9, Q3=16000, Q4=20000, ratio=50 (from the unnamed table — full bore, presumably AC mains powered).\n\nTotal 39 + 1 = 40 models.\n\nModel IDs: \"DN40 class 1\", \"DN40 class 2\", \"DN50 class 1\", \"DN50 class 2\", \"DN65 class 1 & 2\", ... \"DN1800 class 1 & 2\".\n\nShould model entries include attributes like accuracy_class? Let me add {\"model_id\": ..., \"label\": ..., \"attributes\": {...}}? The schema model: model_id, label, attributes{}. I could set label same as model_id, and attributes: {\"accuracy_class\": \"1\"} etc. Hmm — but the values in attributes would then need the strict structured form? Rule 2 says \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object\" — that's for characteristics. Model attributes{} are probably free-form. To keep it simple: attributes: {\"accuracy_class\": \"1\", \"power_supply\": \"battery/renewable energy\", \"bore\": \"full\"}? Hmm, but the same size+class appears in both AC and battery tables (potentially identical values). The instrument variants by power are really configurations.\n\nLet me keep models simple: model_id only, maybe with label. And attributes minimal: {\"accuracy_classes\": ...}? I'll include attributes with accuracy_class string to be helpful:\n\n{\"model_id\": \"DN40 class 1\", \"label\": \"DN40, accuracy class 1\"}\n\nHmm, adding label for all 40 is redundant. Maybe just model_id. And in model_level, I include the accuracy info implicitly. OK: models = [{\"model_id\": \"DN40 class 1\"}, ...]. 40 entries.\n\nHmm, wait — maybe I should reconsider whether to also mark that these flow values are from the battery-powered full-bore table, and DN1800 from AC mains. If the AC mains table on the earlier page has the same values, merger will dedupe by model_id fine. I'll add a note